IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Young Offenders Act 1994 (Western Australia) -- who is a young person (ss 3, 4); what police must consider and may do
-- instead of charging: no action, a caution, or a referral to a juvenile justice team (ss 22B, 22, 22A, 23, 23A, 25, 26, 27,
-- 29, 41); what limits those options (Schedule 1 and Schedule 2 offences, the Weapons Act exclusion, consent); when a team
-- may go ahead and what it may do (ss 30, 31, 32, 37A, 38, 40, 62); what completing the team's terms does (s 33); what is
-- recorded (s 14); and, for the court path, when a conviction is recorded (s 55), the sentencing options as far as s 55
-- needs them (ss 60, 66, 67, 69, 70, 71) and when a conviction stops counting (ss 189, 190).
--
-- SEAT: the young person dealt with by police (Jake, at 15). Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it:
-- "Am I a young person?", "May police caution me instead of charging me?", "May police send me to a juvenile justice team?",
-- "What happens if I am charged after the team?", "Is there anything on my record to spend?". Where the Act hands a judgment
-- to the police officer, the team or the court (s 23's "inappropriate only to give a caution", s 25(2)'s "inappropriate",
-- s 30(3)'s "considers", s 55's "exceptional reasons", s 189(3)'s "special circumstances"), the answer is conditional on
-- that judgment, which is asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 3 ("young person", "independent young person", "responsible adult", "Schedule 1 offence",
-- "Schedule 2 offence"), s 4, s 7(g) (as a comment), s 14(1), (2), s 22A, s 22B, s 22(1), (3), (4), s 23, s 23A(1),
-- s 25(1), (2), (4), (5), s 26(1), s 27, s 29, s 30(1)-(4), s 31(2), s 32(1), (2), (5), (6), s 33, s 37A(2), s 38, s 40,
-- s 41, s 55, s 60, s 62, s 66(1), s 67(1), s 69, s 70, s 71, s 189(1)-(5), (9), s 190(2).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49140, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49140.txt. The deposit names no compilation number. Its compilation table ends with
-- the Community Protection (Offender Reporting) Amendment Act 2024 Pt 3 Div 2 Subdiv 4 (in force 31 December 2025); the
-- last reprint listed is Reprint 7 as at 1 June 2018. The uncommenced provisions table lists, among others, the Young
-- Offenders and Prisons Legislation Amendment Act 2024 Pt 3 (to be proclaimed; not shown, not encoded). No regulations set
-- a number used here; the numbers ($2 000, one year, 2 years, $10 000, 12 months, the ages 17 and 18) are the Act's own.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the full contents of Schedules 1 and 2 (whether an offence is a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence is an
-- INPUT; the comments note the entries the lives touch); whether an infringement notice can be given for an offence (other
-- written laws; an INPUT); s 28 (referral by a court); Part 3 (administration) beyond s 14; Part 4 (custody and bail);
-- ss 42-45 (notices to attend court, explanations, responsible adults in court); Part 7 beyond the provisions listed
-- (conditions and terms of each order, special orders, detention); Parts 8 and 9 (supervised release, detention centres);
-- which court hears a charge (the Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988); and the Spent Convictions Act 1988 itself,
-- which s 189(9) borrows.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA)`

§§ `numbers and helpers`

-- s 3 "young person" (a): "a person who has not reached the age of 18 years".
`the age of adulthood` MEANS 18

-- s 3 "independent young person" (a): "has reached the age of 17 years".
`the age of independence` MEANS 17

-- s 189(2): "a period of 2 years has expired".
`the years before a youth conviction stops counting` MEANS 2

-- s 71: "impose a fine not exceeding $2 000".
`the largest fine instead of imprisonment` MEANS 2000

-- ss 69, 70: "for a term not exceeding one year".
`the longest good behaviour term in months` MEANS 12

GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE
      `on`      IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`age in completed years` MEANS
    IF     DATE_MONTH `on` GREATER THAN DATE_MONTH `born on`
        OR (DATE_MONTH `on` EQUALS DATE_MONTH `born on` AND DATE_DAY `on` AT LEAST DATE_DAY `born on`)
    THEN DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on`
    ELSE DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on` MINUS 1

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE
      `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is on or after` MEANS Day `a` AT LEAST Day `b`

-- The same day and month, a number of years later. (A 29 February start is not handled; none arises here.)
GIVEN `d` IS A DATE
      `n` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`years after` MEANS Date (DATE_DAY `d`) (DATE_MONTH `d`) (DATE_YEAR `d` PLUS `n`)

§§ `3, 4 -- am I a young person`

-- s 3: "young person means -- (a) a person who has not reached the age of 18 years; or (b) a person to whom this Act applies
-- because of section 4". s 4: "If a person commits or allegedly commits an offence before reaching the age of 18 years, this
-- Act applies to the person as a young person for purposes connected with that offence or any order that was made in dealing
-- with the person for that offence."
-- So the test is the age on the day of the offence. A man of 40 is still a young person for an offence alleged at 15.
@export Am I a young person under the Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) for this offence (ss 3, 4)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was (or is said to have been) committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a young person for this offence` IF
    `age in completed years` `my date of birth` `the date of the offence` LESS THAN `the age of adulthood`

-- s 3: "independent young person means a young person who -- (a) has reached the age of 17 years; and (b) has sufficient
-- maturity to live independently without the guidance or control of a responsible adult, and is doing so".
-- "responsible adult ... means a parent, guardian, or other person having responsibility for the day to day care of the
-- young person".
@export Am I an independent young person (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 3)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day the question is asked (for example, the day of the team meeting)
      `I am mature enough to live independently and I do` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have the maturity to live without a responsible adult's guidance or control, and are doing so
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an independent young person` IF
        `age in completed years` `my date of birth` `on` AT LEAST `the age of independence`
    AND `I am mature enough to live independently and I do`

§§ `the matter`

-- s 3: "Schedule 1 offence" and "Schedule 2 offence" are offences against an enactment in column 1 of the Schedule as
-- described in column 2, or counselling or procuring one. Both headings say they are offences "for which a caution cannot be
-- given, and which cannot be referred to a juvenile justice team, and for which a conviction will normally be recorded".
-- Entries the lives touch: plain stealing (The Criminal Code s 378) is in neither Schedule; Schedule 2 lists s 378(2)
-- ("Stealing a motor vehicle, aggravated by reckless or dangerous driving") and s 392 ("Robbery").
DECLARE `Schedule status` IS ONE OF
    `in neither Schedule`
    `a Schedule 1 offence`
    `a Schedule 2 offence`

-- One alleged offence by a young person, as that person could describe it. The officer's judgments are asked as facts.
DECLARE `My matter` HAS
    `my date of birth`                                                    IS A DATE
    `the date of the offence`                                             IS A DATE
    `where the offence sits in the Schedules`                             IS A `Schedule status`
    `it is a Weapons Act 1999 s 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2) offence` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 22(3)(b)
    `there is enough evidence to charge me`                               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 22(1) "could charge"; s 27 "sufficient evidence"
    `the officer judges my previous offences make a caution alone inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 23(1), (2): a judgment
    `an infringement notice can be given for it`                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 25(2): other written laws
    `the officer judges an infringement notice inappropriate here`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 25(2): a judgment
    `I accept responsibility for what I did`                              IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 25(4)
    `I agree to a team rather than a court`                               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 25(4)
    `every potential participant agrees to a team`                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 25(5)
    `I have previously offended, not counting cautions, infringement notices or team matters` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 29(1), (2)
    `I have been cautioned or been to a team before`                      IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 29(2): read by nothing

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is scheduled` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the matter`'s `where the offence sits in the Schedules`
    WHEN `in neither Schedule` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I am a young person for it` MEANS
    `Am I a young person for this offence` (`the matter`'s `my date of birth`) (`the matter`'s `the date of the offence`)

§§ `22A, 22B, 41 -- what police must consider`

-- s 22A: the purpose is "diverting a child who commits an offence from the courts' criminal justice system by allowing a
-- police officer to administer a caution to the young person instead of starting a proceeding for the offence."
-- s 22B: "A police officer, before starting a proceeding against a young person for an offence, must first consider whether
-- in all the circumstances it would be more appropriate -- (a) to take no action; or (b) administer a caution to the young
-- person." s 41 adds a referral to a team, a charge without custody, and arrest. s 7(g): "consideration should be given ...
-- to the possibility of taking measures other than judicial proceedings".
-- The duty is to CONSIDER. It holds even where a caution is barred (a Schedule offence): no action must still be considered.
@export Must police consider taking no action, or cautioning me, before charging me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 22B)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must police consider no action or a caution before charging me` IF
    `I am a young person for it` `the matter`

-- s 41: a member of the Police Force "is to consider whether, in the circumstances, it is appropriate -- (a) to give the
-- person a caution for the offence in accordance with section 22; (b) under section 27, to refer the matter for consideration
-- by a juvenile justice team; (c) to charge the person without taking the person into custody; (d) to apprehend, charge and
-- -- (i) subject to the Bail Act 1982 and section 19(2), detain the person in custody pending the person's appearance in
-- court; or (ii) release the person pending the consideration of the matter by a juvenile justice team."
-- Whether detention is lawful under the Bail Act 1982 is not encoded: options (c) and (d) are open whenever a charge could be.
DECLARE `Police option` IS ONE OF
    `take no action`
    `give me a caution`
    `refer me to a juvenile justice team`
    `charge me without taking me into custody`
    `arrest and charge me`

@export Is this option open to police in dealing with me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) ss 22B, 41)?
GIVEN `the option` IS A `Police option` @desc What police might do
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this option open to police` IF
    CONSIDER `the option`
    WHEN `take no action` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `give me a caution` THEN `May police caution me instead of charging me` `the matter`
    WHEN `refer me to a juvenile justice team` THEN `May police refer me to a juvenile justice team` `the matter`
    OTHERWISE `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge me`

§§ `22, 23, 23A -- cautions`

-- s 22(1): "Where circumstances arise in which a member of the Police Force could charge a young person with the commission
-- of an offence, the member of the Police Force may, having regard to the circumstances, caution the person instead of laying
-- a charge." (3): not if "(a) a Schedule 1 offence or a Schedule 2 offence; or (b) an offence under the Weapons Act 1999
-- section 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2)."
@export May police caution me instead of charging me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 22)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May police caution me instead of charging me` IF
        `I am a young person for it` `the matter`
    AND `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge me`
    AND (NOT `it is scheduled` `the matter`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `it is a Weapons Act 1999 s 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2) offence`)

-- s 23(1): police are "to use the power to caution in preference to laying a charge against a young person unless because of
-- the number of previous offences with which the person has been charged or for which the person has been dealt with under
-- this Part it would be inappropriate only to give a caution." (2): the seriousness of the offence and of previous offences
-- counts. The judgment is the officer's (asked as a fact), and it looks BACK, to offences already charged or dealt with.
-- The preference is over LAYING A CHARGE. A referral (s 27) is made "instead of laying a charge", so s 23 does not rank a
-- caution above a referral.
@export Should police caution me rather than charge me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 23)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Should police caution me rather than charge me` IF
        `May police caution me instead of charging me` `the matter`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `the officer judges my previous offences make a caution alone inappropriate`)

-- s 23A(1): "If a caution is administered to a young person for an offence, the police officer who administered the caution
-- must give the young person a certificate in a form approved by the Commissioner." (2)(g): it states "the nature and effect
-- of a caution". (3): the certificate is evidence, in a proceeding, that the caution was given.
@export Must I be given a caution certificate (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 23A)?
GIVEN `I was cautioned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a police officer cautioned you for the offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be given a caution certificate` IF `I was cautioned`

-- s 22(4): "If a caution is given any admission made by the person cautioned at or about the time the caution is given is not
-- admissible in civil or other proceedings as evidence of any matter to which the caution refers."
-- s 25(4): if the team cannot agree or refers the young person to court, his acceptance of responsibility "is not to be
-- construed as an admission that the offence was committed or a plea of guilty, or otherwise used in evidence against the
-- offender."
DECLARE `What I said` IS ONE OF
    `an admission at or about the time of my caution`
    `my acceptance of responsibility to go to a team`
    `an admission made at some other time`

@export Can what I said be used as evidence against me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) ss 22(4), 25(4))?
GIVEN `what I said` IS A `What I said` @desc What you admitted, and when
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can what I said be used as evidence against me` IF
    CONSIDER `what I said`
    WHEN `an admission made at some other time` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `25, 26, 27, 29 -- referral to a juvenile justice team`

-- s 27: "Where there is sufficient evidence to justify charging a young person with the commission of an offence, a person who
-- could lay the charge may, having regard to the circumstances, refer the matter for consideration by a juvenile justice team
-- instead of laying a charge."
-- s 25(1): not if "a Schedule 1 offence or a Schedule 2 offence". (4): only if the alleged offender "accepts responsibility for
-- the act or omission constituting the offence, and agrees to having the matter dealt with by a juvenile justice team rather
-- than by a court". (5): not "if any one potential participant in the particular proceedings of the team does not agree".
-- The Weapons Act bar in s 22(3)(b) is NOT repeated in s 25: a weapons offence that cannot be cautioned can be referred.
@export May police refer me to a juvenile justice team instead of charging me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) ss 25, 27)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May police refer me to a juvenile justice team` IF
        `I am a young person for it` `the matter`
    AND `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge me`
    AND (NOT `it is scheduled` `the matter`)
    AND `the matter`'s `I accept responsibility for what I did`
    AND `the matter`'s `I agree to a team rather than a court`
    AND `the matter`'s `every potential participant agrees to a team`

-- s 25(2): "If an offence is one for which an infringement notice can be given, the giving of an infringement notice for the
-- offence is to be preferred to referring the matter to a juvenile justice team unless there are circumstances that make the
-- giving of an infringement notice inappropriate."
@export Should I get an infringement notice rather than a team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 25(2))?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Should I get an infringement notice rather than a team` IF
        `the matter`'s `an infringement notice can be given for it`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `the officer judges an infringement notice inappropriate here`)

-- s 29(1): "The discretion given by section 27 or 28 is to be exercised in favour of referring the matter to a juvenile
-- justice team if the young person has not previously offended against the law." (2): a young person is not "taken to have
-- previously offended against the law merely because he or she -- (a) has been cautioned under section 22; or (ba) has been
-- given an infringement notice ...; or (b) has accepted responsibility ... under section 25(4); or (c) has agreed to comply or
-- has complied with the terms specified by a juvenile justice team". The input already leaves those out; a past caution or
-- team matter is recorded separately and changes nothing.
@export Must police favour sending me to a juvenile justice team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 29)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc The offence police say you committed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must police favour sending me to a team` IF
        `May police refer me to a juvenile justice team` `the matter`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `I have previously offended, not counting cautions, infringement notices or team matters`)

-- s 26(1): "If a young person has been arrested for an offence and the matter is referred for consideration by a juvenile
-- justice team, the young person is to be released as soon as is practicable." (2): not to be held "by reason only of the
-- need to decide whether or not to refer the matter".
@export Must I be released if I was arrested and my matter is sent to a team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 26)?
GIVEN `I was arrested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were arrested for the offence
      `my matter was referred to a team` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter has been referred to a juvenile justice team
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be released as soon as practicable` IF
    `I was arrested` AND `my matter was referred to a team`

§§ `30, 31, 32, 38 -- what happens at the team`

-- s 30(1): the team "is to give to a responsible adult notice ... and it can only proceed if a responsible adult is present and
-- has indicated agreement with the proposal and a willingness to participate". (3): if the team "considers the young person
-- to be an independent young person, it may dispense with the requirements of subsection (1)" -- and an independent young
-- person must be 17 (s 3). (4)-(5): if no responsible adult can be found, or notice would be inappropriate, the team may
-- appoint a parent, relative, employer or other suitable adult "to act in place of a responsible adult".
-- s 30(2): a responsible adult who is present must agree to the way the matter is disposed of. s 31(2): a victim who is present
-- must agree "(a) the matter being disposed of by the team; and (b) the way in which the team disposes of the matter."
-- s 32(2): if the young person "is not present ... and does not have a good reason", or a party withdraws agreement "or will
-- not agree to terms specified by the team", the team "is to send the matter back". s 32(6): the team may send it back if it
-- considers a caution or court the right course. s 38: decisions are to be unanimous; if not, the Coordinator sends it back.
DECLARE `My team meeting` HAS
    `the date of the meeting`                                         IS A DATE
    `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 30(1)
    `the team appointed an adult in place of a responsible adult`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 30(4)
    `the team considers I live independently with sufficient maturity` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 30(3) with s 3(b): a judgment
    `the responsible adult present agrees to the way of disposal`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 30(2)
    `a victim is present`                                             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 31(2)
    `the victim agrees to the team and to the way of disposal`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 31(2)
    `I am present or have a good reason not to be`                    IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 32(2)
    `I agree to the terms the team specifies`                         IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 32(1), (2)
    `the team members are unanimous`                                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 38
    `the team thinks a caution or a court is the right course`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 32(6)

DECLARE `Team result` IS ONE OF
    `the team cannot go ahead without a responsible adult`
    `the matter goes back to whoever referred it`
    `the team settles the terms and invites me to comply`

@export Can the juvenile justice team go ahead with me (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 30)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the meeting` IS A `My team meeting` @desc Who attended the team meeting and what they agreed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the team go ahead with me` IF
        `the meeting`'s `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part`
    OR  `the meeting`'s `the team appointed an adult in place of a responsible adult`
    OR  `Am I an independent young person` `my date of birth` (`the meeting`'s `the date of the meeting`) (`the meeting`'s `the team considers I live independently with sufficient maturity`)

@export What happens to my matter at the juvenile justice team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) ss 30-32, 38)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the meeting` IS A `My team meeting` @desc Who attended the team meeting and what they agreed
GIVETH A `Team result`
DECIDE `What happens to my matter at the team` IS
    IF (NOT `Can the team go ahead with me` `my date of birth` `the meeting`)
    THEN `the team cannot go ahead without a responsible adult`
    ELSE IF     (NOT `the meeting`'s `I am present or have a good reason not to be`)
             OR (NOT `the meeting`'s `I agree to the terms the team specifies`)
             OR (`the meeting`'s `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part` AND (NOT `the meeting`'s `the responsible adult present agrees to the way of disposal`))
             OR (`the meeting`'s `a victim is present` AND (NOT `the meeting`'s `the victim agrees to the team and to the way of disposal`))
             OR (NOT `the meeting`'s `the team members are unanimous`)
             OR `the meeting`'s `the team thinks a caution or a court is the right course`
         THEN `the matter goes back to whoever referred it`
         ELSE `the team settles the terms and invites me to comply`

-- s 32(1): the team "may determine the way in which it considers the matter should be disposed of and invite the young person
-- to comply with terms to be specified by the team". (5): it "cannot make an order for restitution or compensation but is to
-- make a record of, and may have regard to, an undertaking or agreement as to any such matter."
-- The Act names no kinds of term; an apology and community work are read (INFERENCE) as terms the team may specify.
DECLARE `Team term` IS ONE OF
    `an apology`
    `restitution or compensation I undertake to make`
    `community work`
    `an order that I pay restitution or compensation`

@export May the juvenile justice team's terms include this (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 32)?
GIVEN `the term` IS A `Team term` @desc What the team might ask of you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the team's terms include this` IF
    CONSIDER `the term`
    WHEN `an order that I pay restitution or compensation` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 37A(2): "During the consideration of a matter by a juvenile justice team, a participant in the proceedings of the team is
-- not to be represented in those proceedings by a lawyer or other agent."
@export May a lawyer represent me at the juvenile justice team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 37A)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a lawyer represent me at the team` IS FALSE

-- s 40(1): no one is to publish or broadcast "any report of the proceedings of a juvenile justice team or any information
-- likely to identify any young person as a person dealt with by a juvenile justice team". (2)(b): "a fine of $10 000 or
-- imprisonment for 12 months".
@export What is the most a publisher who identifies me as dealt with by a team can be fined, in dollars (s 40)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most a publisher who identifies me can be fined` IS 10000

-- s 62: a person required by "terms specified by a juvenile justice team, to perform any work" who "is lawfully engaged in
-- remunerative employment" is, for the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, "to be regarded as a worker
-- employed by the Crown" in respect of that work.
@export Am I treated as a Crown worker for workers compensation while doing the team's work (s 62)?
GIVEN `I am lawfully in paid employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a lawful paid job at the time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a Crown worker while doing the team's work` IF `I am lawfully in paid employment`

§§ `33 -- can I still be charged after the team`

-- s 33(1): "Referral of a matter to a juvenile justice team instead of laying a charge does not prevent a charge from being
-- subsequently laid". (2): "If a young person has complied with the terms specified by a juvenile justice team dealing with
-- the person for an offence, a court hearing a charge of the offence, upon being satisfied that the person has complied with
-- the terms, must dismiss the charge without determining it." (3): if not complied, the court, satisfied that he "has taken
-- steps towards complying", "is to have regard to that, and the extent to which the person did comply".
-- So completing the terms does not bar a charge; it makes the court dismiss one undetermined.
DECLARE `Court outcome` IS ONE OF
    `the charge must be dismissed without being determined`
    `the court deals with me, taking account of the steps I took`
    `the court deals with me`

@export Can I still be charged after a referral to a juvenile justice team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 33(1))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I still be charged after a team referral` IS TRUE

@export What must a court do if I am charged after the juvenile justice team (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 33)?
GIVEN `I complied with the team's terms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did everything the team's terms asked
      `I took steps towards complying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did some of it
GIVETH A `Court outcome`
DECIDE `What must a court do with a later charge` IS
    IF `I complied with the team's terms`
    THEN `the charge must be dismissed without being determined`
    ELSE IF `I took steps towards complying`
         THEN `the court deals with me, taking account of the steps I took`
         ELSE `the court deals with me`

§§ `14 -- what is recorded`

-- s 14(1): "The chief executive officer is to keep records of every young person who is detained or who is dealt with under
-- this Act for an offence." (2): including "(b) details of any detention or any way in which the young person has been dealt
-- with for an offence", "(c) records of every finding by the court that a young person is guilty ... and every conviction",
-- and "(d) details as to the way in which -- (i) terms specified by a juvenile justice team ... have been, complied with".
-- (3): regulations may provide for destruction of records (not retrieved).
-- A caution is given under Part 5 of this Act, so (INFERENCE) a cautioned young person is "dealt with under this Act"; where
-- police take no action, nothing has been done under this Act.
DECLARE `How my matter ended` IS ONE OF
    `police took no action`
    `a caution`
    `a juvenile justice team, terms completed`
    `a charge dismissed under s 33(2)`
    `a finding of guilt without a conviction recorded`
    `a finding of guilt with a conviction recorded`

@export Does the Department keep a record of how my matter was dealt with (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 14)?
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my matter ended` @desc How your matter was dealt with
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Department keep a record of my matter` IF
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `police took no action` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `55, 60, 66, 67, 69-71 -- the court path: is a conviction recorded`

-- s 60 lists what counts as a sentence: refraining from punishment (Divisions 2-4), a fine (Division 5), a youth community based
-- order (Division 6), an intensive youth supervision order or conditional release order (Division 7), a custodial sentence
-- (Division 8). Division 9 (special orders for repeat serious offenders) is not encoded.
-- Only Division 8 is read (INFERENCE from the Division headings) as a "custodial sentence" for s 55.
DECLARE `Sentence` IS ONE OF
    `no punishment and no conditions (s 66)`
    `no punishment, on undertakings or informal punishment (s 67)`
    `no punishment, on a good behaviour recognisance or security (ss 69, 70)`
    `a fine (s 71)`
    `a youth community based order (Division 6)`
    `an intensive youth supervision order (Division 7)`
    `a custodial sentence (Division 8)`

-- s 55(1): if the court finds a young person guilty of a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence, or of any offence and imposes a
-- custodial sentence, it "is required to record a conviction unless it is prevented from doing so by subsection (5) or it is
-- satisfied that there are exceptional reasons for not doing so." (2): otherwise, "the court is not to record a conviction
-- unless it is satisfied that there are exceptional reasons for doing so." (5): "If a young person is found guilty of an
-- offence and, under section 66 or 67, the court refrains from imposing any punishment, the court is not to record a
-- conviction." s 55(5) names ss 66 and 67 only; refraining under s 69 or s 70 falls under s 55(1) or (2).
@export Will a conviction be recorded against me if I am found guilty (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 55)?
GIVEN `the schedule` IS A `Schedule status` @desc Whether the offence is in Schedule 1 or Schedule 2
      `the sentence` IS A `Sentence` @desc What the court does with you
      `the court finds exceptional reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied there are exceptional reasons to depart from the usual rule
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will a conviction be recorded against me` IF
    CONSIDER `the sentence`
    WHEN `no punishment and no conditions (s 66)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `no punishment, on undertakings or informal punishment (s 67)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a custodial sentence (Division 8)` THEN (NOT `the court finds exceptional reasons`)
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the schedule`
        WHEN `in neither Schedule` THEN `the court finds exceptional reasons`
        OTHERWISE (NOT `the court finds exceptional reasons`)

-- s 71: "If the offence is punishable by imprisonment, the court may, instead of sentencing the offender to imprisonment,
-- impose a fine not exceeding $2 000." s 72: only if the payer "has the means to pay".
@export What is the most I can be fined instead of imprisonment, in dollars (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 71)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined instead of imprisonment` IS `the largest fine instead of imprisonment`

-- s 55(4): "Although a conviction is not recorded, the offender is deemed to have been convicted for the purpose of the
-- making ... of any order that may be, or is required to be, made upon convicting a person of such an offence or for the
-- purpose of the operation of any provision of a road law ... relating to the cancellation of, or disqualification from
-- holding or obtaining, a driver's licence ... or for the purpose of an appeal under the Criminal Appeals Act 2004 (but not for
-- any other purpose)."
DECLARE `Purpose` IS ONE OF
    `making an order that follows a conviction`
    `a road law cancelling or disqualifying a driver's licence`
    `an appeal under the Criminal Appeals Act 2004`
    `answering a question about convictions`

@export Am I treated as convicted for this purpose, though no conviction was recorded (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 55(4))?
GIVEN `the purpose` IS A `Purpose` @desc What the question of conviction is being asked for
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I treated as convicted for this purpose` IF
    CONSIDER `the purpose`
    WHEN `answering a question about convictions` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `189, 190 -- when a youth conviction stops counting`

-- s 189(1): not for "murder, attempt to murder or manslaughter". (2): "If a young person is convicted of an offence and a
-- period of 2 years has expired since -- (a) the discharge of any sentence imposed as a result of the conviction, or every
-- sentence ...; or (b) the date of conviction, if no sentence that required to be discharged was imposed", the conviction "is
-- not to be regarded as a conviction for any purpose, except as provided in this section." (3): on application the court, "if
-- it thinks that special circumstances exist", may declare (2) applies early. (4): an order fully complied with is a
-- discharged sentence. (5): if a youth community based order is made, "unless the person has been subsequently dealt with
-- for that offence the conviction is not to be regarded as a conviction for any purpose". (9): "Part 3 of the Spent
-- Convictions Act 1988 has effect in relation to a conviction that, under this section, is not to be regarded as a
-- conviction as if it were a spent conviction under that Act."
DECLARE `My conviction` HAS
    `it is for murder, attempted murder or manslaughter`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 189(1)
    `the date of conviction`                                    IS A DATE
    `a sentence had to be discharged`                           IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 189(2)(a), (b)
    `the date every sentence was discharged`                    IS A DATE     -- s 189(2)(a), (4)
    `a youth community based order was made`                    IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 189(5)
    `I was later dealt with again for that offence`             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 189(5), (7)(a)
    `the court declared special circumstances under s 189(3)`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 189(3): a judgment

@export Has my youth conviction stopped counting as a conviction (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 189)?
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc The conviction and its sentence
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day the question is asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my youth conviction stopped counting` IF
        (NOT `the conviction`'s `it is for murder, attempted murder or manslaughter`)
    AND (    `the conviction`'s `the court declared special circumstances under s 189(3)`
         OR  (`the conviction`'s `a youth community based order was made` AND (NOT `the conviction`'s `I was later dealt with again for that offence`))
         OR  (    `the conviction`'s `a sentence had to be discharged`
              AND `is on or after` `on` (`years after` (`the conviction`'s `the date every sentence was discharged`) `the years before a youth conviction stops counting`))
         OR  (    (NOT `the conviction`'s `a sentence had to be discharged`)
              AND `is on or after` `on` (`years after` (`the conviction`'s `the date of conviction`) `the years before a youth conviction stops counting`)))

-- What there is, under this Act, to spend or to disclose. Only a recorded conviction is a conviction: a caution, a team, a
-- charge dismissed under s 33(2) and a finding of guilt without a conviction (s 55(2), (5)) leave none (INFERENCE: s 189 and
-- s 55 speak only of convictions). Whether a job form's question reaches a caution, a team or a finding of guilt depends on
-- the form's words and on the Spent Convictions Act 1988, which is not encoded.
DECLARE `What there is to spend` IS ONE OF
    `nothing: no conviction was recorded`
    `a conviction that no longer counts, treated as spent under the Spent Convictions Act Part 3`
    `a conviction that still counts`

@export Is there a conviction from my matter to spend or disclose (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) ss 55, 189)?
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How my matter ended` @desc How your matter was dealt with
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc The conviction and its sentence, if a conviction was recorded (otherwise ignored)
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day the question is asked
GIVETH A `What there is to spend`
DECIDE `Is there a conviction from my matter to spend` IS
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `a finding of guilt with a conviction recorded` THEN
        IF `Has my youth conviction stopped counting` `the conviction` `on`
        THEN `a conviction that no longer counts, treated as spent under the Spent Convictions Act Part 3`
        ELSE `a conviction that still counts`
    OTHERWISE `nothing: no conviction was recorded`

-- s 190(2): "A person, not being the person convicted, who discloses the fact of a conviction that, under section 189, is not
-- to be regarded as a conviction commits an offence except if the disclosure is for the purposes of this Act or of any court
-- of law." (1): evidence of it is inadmissible except in proceedings under this Act or on indictment for that or a later
-- offence.
@export May someone else disclose my youth conviction (Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) s 190(2))?
GIVEN `it has stopped counting under s 189` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conviction is no longer to be regarded as a conviction
      `for this Act or a court of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure is for the purposes of this Act or of a court
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May someone else disclose my youth conviction` IF
        (NOT `it has stopped counting under s 189`)
    OR  `for this Act or a court of law`
